 parts:[
 p('State whether the result is a document or a reading','The request asks for assets with a qualifying reading. Its output contains each asset once. A different request might ask for every matching reading, in which case several results for one asset are useful.\n\nDecide that result grain first. A database can match a whole document because one nested member qualifies, then return the entire document or a chosen projection. Filtering the parent does not automatically remove its other array members.',null,'A matching asset and its matching readings are different result shapes.'),
 p('Follow an ordinary field path','A path such as sensor.latest identifies a nested field when sensor is an object. A path through an array needs additional semantics because there may be several candidate members. In MongoDB, ordinary dotted conditions on array fields can be satisfied by different members unless the query binds them together appropriately.\n\nThat distinction explains the P1 error. The document contains a temperature member and a high-value member, but the pair we need does not exist.',null,'Keep the member identity until all conditions referring to that member have been tested.'),
 p('Trace one member through both checks','Expand P1’s array into two rows, each still carrying P1. Keep only temperature rows. Its 65 remains, and the 95 vibration row leaves. Now apply the threshold: the remaining P1 row fails. P2’s 85 passes.\n\nThis is an explanatory construction. A database can use another execution plan while preserving the requested same-member meaning. We are learning the semantics before discussing its access cost.',null,'The order of these two pure filters can change without changing the selected members.'),
 {...p('Bind the conditions with $elemMatch','MongoDB’s $elemMatch requires at least one member of the specified array to match all the criteria inside it. Put the kind and threshold in the same member condition.\n\nThe query object below is supplied to find. Its value is a data structure, not a string to splice together from untrusted fragments. Applications should build allowed fields and operators deliberately rather than accepting an arbitrary query object from a client.',null,'A correct predicate still needs tenant and resource scoping in the service that executes it.'),references:[ref('A same-member reading predicate',urls.elem,'json','{"readings":{"$elemMatch":{"kind":"temperature","value":{"$gte":80}}}}','Pass this query object to a MongoDB collection find operation containing the lesson’s readings arrays.','Use a disposable MongoDB collection outside the browser. The workshop explicitly expands rows instead of parsing this JSON.','P1 with temperature 65 and vibration 95 does not match; P2 with temperature 85 does.')]},
 p('Return the fields the screen needs','Projection chooses which fields appear in the returned documents. If the screen only needs asset identities, avoid transferring complete histories. In MongoDB an inclusion projection ordinarily includes _id unless it is explicitly excluded. Inclusion and exclusion rules have restrictions, so use the documented form.\n\nProjection does not make private records public. Authorise the query separately, then reduce the returned fields. In our expanded workshop, projection leaves asset_id and deduplication removes repeated matches for the same asset.',null,'Deduplication changes the output grain after the member checks are complete.'),
 p('Treat missing and null as explicit query choices','In MongoDB, equality to null can match an explicitly null field or a missing field. Use the appropriate existence and type conditions when those states mean different things. That differs from simply carrying over SQL’s equality behaviour for NULL.\n\nOur reading fixture requires a numeric value in every member. The challenge therefore does not pretend to reproduce MongoDB type comparison, collation or null semantics. If your production records allow malformed members, decide whether to reject them at ingestion, exclude them, or report them separately.',null,'Make the dataset contract as explicit as the predicate.'),
 p('Write the member test directly in Python','Python any answers whether at least one member passes. The condition inside it reads kind and value from the same reading dictionary. An empty array contains no qualifying member.\n\nThe function returns asset IDs in input order. That is our worksheet contract; a real database query needs an explicit sort when presentation order matters.',null,'One member supplies both values to the test.',[example('Inspect the two conditions for each member','A shared loop variable prevents a high vibration value from being attached to the temperature label.','readings = [{"kind":"temperature","value":65},{"kind":"vibration","value":95}]\nfor reading in readings:\n    qualifies = reading["kind"] == "temperature" and reading["value"] >= 80\n    print(reading["kind"], qualifies)','temperature False\nvibration False','Neither member qualifies, even though the separate kind and threshold conditions both appear somewhere in the array.')]),
 p('Keep the case that split the evidence','The first workshop fixture has only temperature readings. A shortcut that separately checks the presence of temperature and any high value may pass it. The changed fixture adds a high vibration value beside a low temperature. That is the case that distinguishes the intended predicate.\n\nAlso keep an empty array and an asset with several qualifying members. Those test absence and output grain, neither of which the initial fixture establishes.',null,'The next lesson changes a document. A correct match must remain part of the write itself when another editor can act concurrently.'),
 ],checkpointAfter:4,
